1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,340
The talk right now that you're in is reflections on trusting trust zone by Dan Rosenberg. Sorry,

2
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:13,340
Rosenberg. Thank you. All right. So as you said, I'm Dan. And today we're going to be talking

3
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:18,760
about attacking trust zone. So hopefully if you're in this room you've heard of trust zone

4
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:24,200
before or have some idea of what it is. But just in case, trust zone is a system wide approach

5
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,300
to security implemented on ARM devices which enjoy a sort of majority market share, especially

6
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:35,300
among mobile devices. And Trustzone allows these devices to implement a fairly wide range

7
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:42,300
of security related tasks. It accomplishes this by segregating all software and hardware on the

8
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:47,180
device into what are referred to as the normal world and the secure world. So in the normal

9
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:52,720
world you have all of the components of your traditional operating system including your

10
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:57,100
standard applications, system services, and the operating system kernel. Now today we're

11
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:00,940
going to be talking exclusively about an implementation on Android. So this will include the

12
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:06,720
Linux kernel for example. Then in contrast on the other side we have the secure world. Now

13
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:11,300
there are a number of different design models for implementing a secure world. But the most

14
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:17,220
popular in practice is having a secure operating system kernel or a trust zone kernel under which

15
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:23,060
a number of potentially independent trusted applications or trustlets may run. And these can

16
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:28,260
run concurrently and are frequently written by third parties to implement various modularized

17
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:34,400
pieces of security technology. Now, access between the normal world and the secure world is

18
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:40,360
mediated by a monitor component. And the secure world has complete access to all of the hardware

19
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:45,860
and software on the device. But the normal world does not. It can only communicate with the

20
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:51,400
secure world via carefully defined channels. So the secure world is capable of designating

21
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:56,900
certain regions of memory for example as secure only and the normal world even with higher

22
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:02,740
privileges like kernel privileges cannot access those secure memory segments. So it's acting

23
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:10,740
much like a hypervisor in this case. In my experience the most common usage of trust zone is for

24
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:17,780
DRM, digital rights management, where the basic use case is protected media content can be sent

25
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:23,980
to a user's device and then decoded exclusively in the secure world. So even if the user has

26
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:30,480
full control over their own operating system, all of this decoding takes place in a protected

27
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:36,160
environment that the user does not have access to to prevent piracy. Additionally, trust zone

28
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:41,320
is used frequently for other secure key storage applications such as for DM verify which is the

29
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:46,240
Linux kernel's full disk encryption technology. It may also be used for storing sensitive

30
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:52,240
information related to mobile payments and for creating protected hardware components such as

31
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:57,520
a secure keypad that only communicates with the secure world such that if the operating system

32
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:03,520
is compromised perhaps elements of pin entry may remain secure. Additionally, many vendors

33
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:12,700
choose to implement secure boot in such a way that it relies on software programmable fuses such

34
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:18,540
is Qualcomm's QFuse technology. And because TrustZone has the ability to read and write these

35
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:23,460
software programmable fuses, frequently TrustZone plays some sort of role either directly or

36
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:29,040
indirectly in actually managing secure boot itself on mobile devices. And then finally a

37
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:34,120
number of device manufacturers, most notably Samsung, have implemented their own security

38
00:03:34,120 --> 00:03:39,240
solutions that rely on TrustZone. As part of their Knox security suite, Samsung has

39
00:03:39,240 --> 00:03:44,580
implemented TEMA which is the trust zone based integrity measurement architecture. Among other

40
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:50,940
things, this subsystem runs in the secure world and periodically scans the device kernel in

41
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:55,900
memory to verify that no tampering has taken place. And if it detects any sort of compromise or

42
00:03:55,900 --> 00:04:00,160
suspicious activity, it will raise some sort of security exception or potentially reboot the

43
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:07,120
phone. Prior to today, there's actually been very little public research on attacking trust

44
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:13,120
zone. Thomas Roth gave a talk in 2013 I believe at CanSec West called next generation mobile

45
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:20,000
rootkits in which he described using trust zone as a platform for surreptitiously spying on a

46
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,580
user and noted that it's a particularly good choice for this kind of activity because even if a

47
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:30,700
user has the ability to control the kernel, the kernel does not have the ability to look into

48
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:36,580
trust zone to see if any sort of malicious activity is going on. And while he described the

49
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:42,040
existence of specific vulnerabilities in trust zone as a potential vector to install such a

50
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:46,460
root kit, to my knowledge he didn't talk about specific vulnerabilities, much less exploitation

51
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:52,040
of them, which is what I'm going to be mostly focusing on today. Next, also in 2013, I

52
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:57,040
published a post on the Azimuth security blog called unlocking the Motorola boot loader in

53
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,820
which I described a Motorola specific trust zone vulnerability that allowed me to defeat

54
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:08,900
secure boot on these devices. Next, a number of exploits published by the sort of Android

55
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:14,160
routing and modding community, like XDA developers and the like, specifically for HTC

56
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:20,160
phones, have used trust zone related attacks in the past, but these exploits have all been not

57
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:27,240
only undocumented but obfuscated and not publicly analyzed, so very little public insight has

58
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:33,240
been gleaned by this research. And then most recently, perhaps a month or two ago at

59
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:37,860
recon, Nathan Keltner and Charles Holmes gave a talk called here be dragons where they

60
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:44,620
described vulnerabilities specific to HTC's trust and implementation. And I should thank these

61
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:50,860
guys for communicating with me while they were doing this research and sharing their findings.

62
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:54,660
So looking back at the previous couple slides, we can begin to paint a picture of why this may

63
00:05:54,660 --> 00:05:59,900
be an interesting research target. Firstly, it's clearly a high value target to a number

64
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:05,160
of different parties. You have content providers who rely on trust zone to prevent piracy of

65
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:11,500
their media. You have OEMs like Samsung who implement security services to protect their

66
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:16,820
users and gain advantage especially in enterprise markets. And then we have users who rely on

67
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:22,320
the same security features to protect themselves and as is the case with groups like XDA

68
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:28,160
developers and other ROM and rooting enthusiasts frequently want to defeat trust zone to defeat

69
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:33,440
things like secure boot to install their own software on their phones. This coupled with the

70
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:36,740
fact that there's been very little public research and as a result very little public

71
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:41,360
scrutiny to support the, you know, sort of lofty claims of security that marketing

72
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:47,240
literature has put forward makes this a compelling target. So for this research in

73
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:52,120
particular, I chose to focus on Qualcomm's implementation of a trust zone kernel, which is

74
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:56,700
called the Qualcomm Secure Execution Environment, or QSEE. And the reason behind this was

75
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:02,120
fairly simple. Qualcomm enjoys a sort of majority market share, especially among mid to

76
00:07:02,120 --> 00:07:08,440
high end Android phones, especially in North America and Europe. Basically, if you have

77
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:15,320
heard of a flagship model by Samsung, LG, Motorola, HTC, and it's for sale in Europe or North

78
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:19,860
America, chances are it's running a Qualcomm chipset and as a result is using Qualcomm's

79
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:25,860
trust zone kernel. So it's by far the most popular trust zone kernel in the market today.

80
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:31,860
For actually performing this research, the tool chain requirements are fairly minimal.

81
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:37,620
Obtaining trust zone images is incredibly straightforward. If you just spend the time

82
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:43,200
Google searching, you can find huge archives of firmware images for dozens and dozens of

83
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:49,000
Android devices. And frequently these are just simple archive formats like tar balls or zips.

84
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:54,820
And you can pull out the trust zone images directly from those full firmware file formats.

85
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:59,580
Additionally if you have root access on a live device you can use a Linux utility like DD to

86
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:05,320
simply pull the trust zone image directly from disk. And once you have a trust zone image in

87
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:10,620
hand analysis is really just as straightforward as pulling up your favorite disassembler in my

88
00:08:10,620 --> 00:08:17,320
case IDAPRO and going to work reverse engineering. Now earlier trust zone images actually rely on

89
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:23,580
a proprietary Qualcomm header format which is a 40 byte header that dictates where in memory

90
00:08:23,580 --> 00:08:29,080
certain sections of trust zone are loaded which is fairly easy to sort of guess the meaning of

91
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:32,740
and I think some people have gone out of their way to provide third party documentation on

92
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:38,000
that. But nowadays the more recent trust zone kernels are actually ELF binaries so you can

93
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:43,000
load them up in IDA pro and it will handle loading things in memory for you and then you can go to

94
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:50,000
work. Now I find it's a useful exercise whenever beginning any sort of vulnerability research

95
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:55,660
endeavor to enumerate the attack surface that's present on the target. In this case by far the

96
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:59,660
most lucrative piece of attack surface was a software exception mechanism called the secure

97
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:05,660
monitor call or SMC. So SMC is an ARM instruction which is a privileged instruction meaning it can

98
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:11,280
only be executed by the kernel. That is somewhat analogous to a system call. So much like a

99
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:16,700
normal application will issue a system call to request the operating system kernel provide

100
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:23,160
some sort of service for it, the operating system kernel can issue an SMC request to request

101
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:28,160
that trust zone do something on its behalf. Additionally, trust zone may be tasked with

102
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:32,360
handling various hardware exceptions such as interrupts and external aborts. But this is a

103
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:36,440
much more narrowly defined interface so I suspect it's less likely to have vulnerabilities.

104
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:42,060
Qualcomm's implementation doesn't rely on this so much but other trust's own implementations

105
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:47,660
most notably mobile core rely fairly extensively on shared memory segments to pass information

106
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:52,740
back and forth between the secure and non-secure worlds. So that's a potentially interesting

107
00:09:52,740 --> 00:09:58,440
target on those implementations. Next, it's possibly interesting to note that the contents

108
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:04,940
of the disk, EMC flash on the devices, is a potential attack factor to attack the secure

109
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:09,600
world. When you consider the fact that in many cases TrustZone will actually read the contents

110
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:14,480
of disk and do some amount of parsing, for example, to verify the contents of disk for secure

111
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:19,820
boot, any vulnerabilities in that reading from disk and parsing could potentially be exploited

112
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:25,940
by pathological disk contents to exploit these vulnerabilities. And then finally, there may

113
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:30,820
actually be vulnerabilities in the individual trustlets, these trusted applications that are

114
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:36,020
frequently authored by third parties. And I did spend some time looking at a number of these and

115
00:10:36,020 --> 00:10:42,020
found that there was a high amount of sort of low hanging fruit, somewhat trivial and easy to

116
00:10:42,020 --> 00:10:46,740
exploit vulnerabilities in these trustlets. Probably because the vendors that are authoring

117
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:50,820
them likely don't have the same kind of security engineering culture as an organization like

118
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:56,820
Qualcomm might. But unfortunately, or fortunately, these trustlets are isolated from one another on

119
00:10:59,660 --> 00:11:04,160
later trust zone kernels. So even if you're able to compromise an individual trustlet with a

120
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:08,700
vulnerability in it, you don't necessarily gain access to other trustlets, much less the trust

121
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:13,700
zone kernel. So it's a somewhat more limited attack. Having enumerated this attack surface, I

122
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:19,860
having enumerated this attack surface I wanted to be clear about what my sort of assumptions

123
00:11:19,860 --> 00:11:23,940
were about the capabilities an attacker would need to possess to perform an attack on trust

124
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:30,320
zone. So for today's research I wanted to assume that we had arbitrary code execution on the

125
00:11:30,320 --> 00:11:34,820
device already. And this is really just for practical reasons because there's essentially

126
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:39,620
no remote attack surface for most real trust zone implementations. It's very difficult to

127
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:45,120
imagine a sane use case where the trust zone kernel on a phone needs to receive data directly

128
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:51,320
remotely. That having been said we also want to assume that the attacker has kernel privileges

129
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:56,420
on the device. Whether that be because the attacker is the owner of the phone who actually

130
00:11:56,420 --> 00:12:02,420
physically owns it or an attacker that has gained access to a device and escalated privileges

131
00:12:02,420 --> 00:12:07,740
via a kernel exploit which is a somewhat well understood process. The reason for this

132
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:12,040
assumption is that we really want the ability to issue these SMC instructions because this

133
00:12:12,040 --> 00:12:17,500
provides by far the broadest attack surface. And if we don't have kernel privileges there's

134
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:22,200
practically no ability to interact with trust zone at all. You can sort of imagine a scenario

135
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:28,240
where some sort of unprivileged application may be able to request that a privileged application

136
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:33,360
make a call to trust zone on its behalf that exploits a vulnerability in trust zone. But as

137
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:39,120
you can probably guess this is seeming less and less likely to happen in real life. The last

138
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:44,980
thing that's important to note is that in this threat model crashes and denial of service

139
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:50,240
bugs in trust zone are not considered security relevant to us. Because we're assuming that we

140
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:55,200
already have the ability to run code as the kernel, we already have the ability to not

141
00:12:55,200 --> 00:13:01,200
only reboot the device but also probably do irreparable harm to it. So having the kernel

142
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:06,580
trigger something that causes trust zone to crash doesn't actually result in gaining any sort of

143
00:13:06,580 --> 00:13:12,660
privileges that we didn't already have. Next we're going to be moving on to the actual

144
00:13:12,660 --> 00:13:19,240
implementation details of QSE itself specifically. So QSE as a trust zone kernel is closed

145
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:24,360
source. So all of the information presented here on QSE has been reverse engineered in IDA

146
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:32,360
Pro. However, the kernel side of things that actually communicates with TrustZone is open

147
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:40,240
source as part of the forum GitHub tree that's available online. So actually looking at these

148
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:45,600
kernel drivers and how they talk to TrustZone gave some insight on some of the ways of

149
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:53,820
communication. So the interface that Qualcomm provides as a kernel driver is called SCM which

150
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:57,860
is the secure channel manager which I found very confusing considering the fact that it

151
00:13:57,860 --> 00:14:04,860
actually uses the SMC instruction as the way of communication. And the way it issues these

152
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:09,820
requests is via two calling conventions. There is a call by register convention and a

153
00:14:09,820 --> 00:14:15,740
convention that relies on using a request and response structure. So the call by register

154
00:14:15,740 --> 00:14:22,120
convention is fairly straightforward. In this convention the kernel loads the R0 register

155
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:26,700
with this ORD value that contains the SMC command number, which you can think of as a system

156
00:14:26,700 --> 00:14:30,620
call number. It's just sort of the ID of the command that the kernel is requesting of trust

157
00:14:30,620 --> 00:14:35,960
zone. Various flags and then the number of arguments that it should expect. And then the

158
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:41,960
individual arguments to the SMC request go in subsequent ARM registers R1, R2 and so on.

159
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:51,000
Much more interesting is the second convention which relies on these command structures. So in

160
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:55,500
in this convention the R0 register is loaded with the physical address corresponding to a

161
00:14:55,500 --> 00:15:00,920
command buffer and then the SMC instruction is issued. Now these command buffers feature a

162
00:15:00,920 --> 00:15:06,920
request header, a request buffer containing essentially arbitrary input data, a response

163
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:12,760
header, and then a response buffer containing arbitrary output data. The request header is

164
00:15:12,760 --> 00:15:17,380
described by this SCM command structure in the kernel tree that's available as open

165
00:15:17,380 --> 00:15:23,580
source. And it features a length field which describes the length of the entire command buffer.

166
00:15:23,580 --> 00:15:28,920
A buffer offset field which is the offset of the input data from the beginning of the command.

167
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:32,620
The response header offset which is the offset of the response header from the beginning of the

168
00:15:32,620 --> 00:15:39,000
command. The ID which is this ORD value that encapsulates the SMC request number flags and so

169
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:44,380
on. And then the actual input data. And this is expected to be populated by the kernel before it

170
00:15:44,380 --> 00:15:51,380
issues the SMC request to TrustZone. Then when TrustZone has finished processing the request

171
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:59,040
it is expected to populate the SCM response structure which contains a length field, a buffer

172
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:02,880
offset field which is the offset from the beginning of the response header to the output

173
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:09,880
data and then this is complete flag which is just set to 1. Now because this request structure

174
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:16,880
now because this request structure is populated entirely by the kernel and in our threat model

175
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:21,460
from the perspective of trust zone we're considering the kernel as untrusted as a potential

176
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:28,080
attacker then trust zone needs to perform some sanity checking or validation of this request

177
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:32,580
structure to ensure that the kernel is not trying to trick trust zone into doing something

178
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:38,880
malicious into perhaps writing into secure memory and corrupting something. Now in particular

179
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:44,720
there are five sanity checks that they perform to make sure that the request structure is

180
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:49,920
sane. The first of these is that the length of the command buffer is greater than or equal to

181
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:55,540
16 which is the size of the request header. Next they confirm that the buffer offset which is

182
00:16:55,540 --> 00:17:00,000
the offset from the beginning of the command buffer to the input buffer resides somewhere

183
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:05,920
inside the command buffer. All of these make sense so far. Next they check that the buffer

184
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:10,620
offset is greater than or equal to 16, which is the size of the request header. To guarantee

185
00:17:10,620 --> 00:17:16,260
that the input data doesn't overlap with the request header. Next, they confirm that the

186
00:17:16,260 --> 00:17:21,000
response header offset is less than or equal to the command length minus 12, where 12 is the

187
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,880
size of the response header. So this is a check to actually guarantee that the response header

188
00:17:25,880 --> 00:17:32,000
fits inside the command buffer. And then finally, perhaps most importantly, there is an

189
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:37,620
invocation of a function that I called QSEE is non-secure memory. There's no simple support

190
00:17:37,620 --> 00:17:43,120
available in trust zones so all of these functions I just named appropriately for convenience

191
00:17:43,120 --> 00:17:49,080
sake. And what this function is designed to do is guarantee that the entire command buffer

192
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:54,040
being provided by the kernel resides in non-secure memory. And when you think about it, this

193
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:59,880
makes sense. This is an important security assumption. Because if the kernel were to, for

194
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:05,580
example provide an address residing in secure memory and then issue an SMC instruction,

195
00:18:05,580 --> 00:18:10,460
it's possible that TrustZone would then go ahead and perform operations, either read from or

196
00:18:10,460 --> 00:18:15,460
write to that memory in secure memory and potentially allow for a compromise. The way

197
00:18:19,260 --> 00:18:25,300
these secure memory checking functions work, they're actually a series of functions that are

198
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:30,720
designed to check if a range of memory is considered protected. And the way they implement this is

199
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:35,340
entirely in software using a hard coded list of regions with various flags that indicate

200
00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:43,340
various memory attributes. So when this top level secure range checking function is called, QSE

201
00:18:43,340 --> 00:18:48,180
iterates over this list of regions and checks that the specified region doesn't overlap with

202
00:18:48,180 --> 00:18:53,340
anything that's considered protected and returns a value appropriately. And this is somewhat

203
00:18:53,340 --> 00:18:57,640
analogous to the Linux kernel's use of the access okay macro for those of you who are

204
00:18:57,640 --> 00:19:02,420
familiar with Linux programming. Where the question that's being asked is, is this memory

205
00:19:02,420 --> 00:19:07,580
safe for trust zone to operate on? Or is the kernel potentially trying to trick trust zone

206
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:14,720
into doing something malicious? Now, this is pseudocode that I put together that roughly

207
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:20,300
approximates what goes on when QSE performs these checks. You have this top level function,

208
00:19:20,300 --> 00:19:25,800
QSEE is non-secure memory that takes arguments of an address, meaning the start of the region

209
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:30,000
that's going to be checked and a size field indicating how big the region that they want to

210
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:37,000
check is. Next, they call down to a secondary function that I called QSEE range not in region

211
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:41,460
where they provide the address of this linked list of protected regions that resides in secure

212
00:19:41,460 --> 00:19:47,720
memory and then they provide the address again and then they attempt to calculate the end of

213
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:53,420
the region that they want to check by adding the address plus the size of the region. Now

214
00:19:53,420 --> 00:19:58,420
interestingly in this function this secondary function actually checks that if the end of the

215
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:06,420
region that's going to be checked is less than the start of the region that's going to be

216
00:20:06,420 --> 00:20:11,140
checked they assume that the caller has made some kind of an error and they reverse the

217
00:20:11,140 --> 00:20:15,980
arguments for you. And this immediately jumped out at me as something that's potentially a

218
00:20:15,980 --> 00:20:22,860
a very bad idea. So some of you probably have already spotted the bug having looked at that

219
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:30,400
code. Just in case, quick review of the concept of an integer overflow. So on ARM devices or

220
00:20:30,400 --> 00:20:35,220
other 32-bit devices in general, integers are represented as 32-bit numbers which is the

221
00:20:35,220 --> 00:20:41,900
maximum size of a single standard register. So integers have a maximum range of zero to

222
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:47,800
hex F F F F F F F F which is 2 to the 32 minus 1. So for what

223
00:20:47,800 --> 00:20:51,580
during arithmetic operations if this maximum value of an integer

224
00:20:51,580 --> 00:20:55,640
is exceeded then the resulting value will wrap back around to

225
00:20:55,640 --> 00:21:00,220
zero. So in this case if you add the largest integer possible

226
00:21:00,220 --> 00:21:06,360
with one you get zero. So applying this idea to the

227
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:08,620
invocation of the secure memory checking that's used to

228
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:16,620
validate this SCM command structure, they invoke this is not secure memory with an attack

229
00:21:16,620 --> 00:21:21,660
a kernel provided or attacker provided in our case address of a command and the length of that

230
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:27,660
command. Now what happens if the address of the command plus its supposed length both of which

231
00:21:27,660 --> 00:21:33,660
are attacker provided values is greater than the maximum representation of a 32 bit integer?

232
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:40,700
in this case the answer is fairly clear. So this secondary function will be called with the start

233
00:21:40,700 --> 00:21:48,080
of the command buffer and then this address plus size field will actually be will wrap around and

234
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:53,320
result in an address that is lower than the starting address. And then in the secondary

235
00:21:53,320 --> 00:22:00,040
function end will be less than start and it will reverse these arguments. So this allows us to

236
00:22:00,040 --> 00:22:06,920
create a pathological command buffer that does evil things. So in this example, say that this

237
00:22:06,920 --> 00:22:13,920
region here is the actual QSEE image which is secure memory. It's considered protected. Now we can

238
00:22:13,920 --> 00:22:19,920
place our command start perhaps over here and then provide an extremely large command length such

239
00:22:21,920 --> 00:22:27,700
that the sum of the address of the command and command length wraps all the way around and ends

240
00:22:27,700 --> 00:22:33,940
up here. Then in the range checking function these arguments will be reversed so that the

241
00:22:33,940 --> 00:22:39,940
range that is checked to guarantee that it is in non secure memory is this region here rather

242
00:22:39,940 --> 00:22:44,580
than the region starting at command and extending all the way to the end of memory which is what

243
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:50,580
it intended to do. As a result we can actually set a response buffer offset to be anywhere in

244
00:22:50,580 --> 00:22:57,580
offset to be anywhere in this region of memory. Including on top of the QSCE image itself.

245
00:23:01,080 --> 00:23:05,540
So looking through these five sanity checks once more it's clear that with this pathological

246
00:23:05,540 --> 00:23:10,420
command buffer all of these conditions are satisfied. The command length is significantly

247
00:23:10,420 --> 00:23:14,880
greater than 16. The buffer offset is less than the command length. The buffer offset is

248
00:23:14,880 --> 00:23:19,640
significantly greater than 16. The response header offset is our sort of arbitrary offset

249
00:23:19,640 --> 00:23:22,840
to point into the secure memory is less than our command length

250
00:23:22,840 --> 00:23:26,380
which is really massive. And then because of this integer

251
00:23:26,380 --> 00:23:30,060
overflow and the subsequent reversal of these start and end

252
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:33,860
values for range checking, this check that is designed to

253
00:23:33,860 --> 00:23:38,520
guarantee that this range is not secure returns true even though

254
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:43,200
it clearly should be returning false. Now the result of this

255
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:47,700
bug is that we can craft an SMC request that exploits this

256
00:23:47,700 --> 00:23:52,500
integer overflow and causes QSEE to write a response header to

257
00:23:52,500 --> 00:23:56,900
arbitrary secure memory. And this is clearly a serious

258
00:23:56,900 --> 00:23:59,740
vulnerability that can result in compromise. And the goal from

259
00:23:59,740 --> 00:24:02,980
this point forward is to achieve arbitrary secure code

260
00:24:02,980 --> 00:24:08,320
execution on these devices. The first thing to look at is

261
00:24:08,320 --> 00:24:10,820
clearly what gets written to a response header. What kind of

262
00:24:10,820 --> 00:24:14,820
data can we cause QSEE to write to arbitrary secure memory. And

263
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:19,820
turns out that the response header is hard coded in all cases. It

264
00:24:19,820 --> 00:24:23,620
writes the length as 12 which is just the size of the response

265
00:24:23,620 --> 00:24:28,060
header. The buffer offset is 12 and then this is complete is set

266
00:24:28,060 --> 00:24:31,060
to one. So the final result is that we can write these three

267
00:24:31,060 --> 00:24:38,480
words 12, 12, 1 to arbitrary secure memory. Exploiting this is

268
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:42,020
somewhat of a challenge but a few things make it easier. The

269
00:24:42,020 --> 00:24:45,660
first of these is the memory layout of QSE is almost entirely

270
00:24:45,660 --> 00:24:49,520
known to the attacker. Because the trust zone image sits on

271
00:24:49,520 --> 00:24:52,360
disk and we're assuming we're running as the kernel, we can

272
00:24:52,360 --> 00:24:55,760
read that image from disk and because it is loaded at a known

273
00:24:55,760 --> 00:25:00,380
constant physical address in memory, we essentially know

274
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:03,400
every, we know the entire layout of the trust zone image in

275
00:25:03,400 --> 00:25:06,340
memory even though we can't explicitly read the secure

276
00:25:06,340 --> 00:25:10,340
memory where it resides. Next, it's helpful that most of RAM

277
00:25:10,340 --> 00:25:13,640
is actually non-secure memory so we have a somewhat large sort of

278
00:25:13,640 --> 00:25:16,480
scratch pad to work with when we're trying to do things in

279
00:25:16,480 --> 00:25:20,420
memory. Now the sort of classic approach that you would take if

280
00:25:20,420 --> 00:25:24,280
you were approaching this like a traditional kernel exploit maybe

281
00:25:24,280 --> 00:25:27,100
to find some sort of function pointer that lives in secure

282
00:25:27,100 --> 00:25:31,060
memory and then use our write primitive to overwrite some of

283
00:25:31,060 --> 00:25:34,740
the bytes of that function pointer so that it now points to

284
00:25:34,740 --> 00:25:37,540
an address residing in non-secure memory. Then we

285
00:25:37,540 --> 00:25:40,920
could put a payload at that non secure address and cause that

286
00:25:40,920 --> 00:25:44,280
function pointer to be invoked by trust zone and then the result

287
00:25:44,280 --> 00:25:46,880
would be trust zone running our non secure payload and that would

288
00:25:46,880 --> 00:25:52,780
be victory. Unfortunately, this doesn't actually appear to work.

289
00:25:52,780 --> 00:25:56,500
So I suspect that QSEE actually has some sort of mechanism in

290
00:25:56,500 --> 00:25:59,200
place that prevents trust zone from executing code from non

291
00:25:59,200 --> 00:26:02,700
secure pages. Sort of analogously to how user land

292
00:26:02,700 --> 00:26:08,700
applications have NX or DEP or more recently the Linux kernel

293
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:15,640
on ARM or X86 have PXN and SMEP respectively. And I suspect it

294
00:26:15,640 --> 00:26:18,540
may have to do with the XPU which is memory management

295
00:26:18,540 --> 00:26:21,880
component that handles marking regions as secure. But this is

296
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:25,720
an undocumented black hole. It's very difficult to understand

297
00:26:25,720 --> 00:26:28,620
what's going on here and I don't have the capabilities to look

298
00:26:28,620 --> 00:26:30,660
at the hardware close enough to actually understand what's

299
00:26:30,660 --> 00:26:34,260
going on. So this is the part of the talk where I coyly ask if

300
00:26:34,260 --> 00:26:37,000
there are any Qualcomm or ARM employees in the audience who

301
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:40,700
want to answer this question as to whether this is the case but

302
00:26:40,700 --> 00:26:42,740
I'll let them off the hook because I assume they can't

303
00:26:42,740 --> 00:26:48,880
say. So we need to take another approach to actually

304
00:26:48,880 --> 00:26:51,080
gaining arbitrary code execution because it's clear

305
00:26:51,080 --> 00:26:53,480
that at least from experimentation it's not

306
00:26:53,480 --> 00:26:55,980
going to work. We're not going to be able to cause trust zone

307
00:26:55,980 --> 00:26:59,160
to execute non secure code. We need to find a way to cause

308
00:26:59,160 --> 00:27:02,660
trust zone to execute secure code in a way that allows us to

309
00:27:02,660 --> 00:27:08,660
win. Now the fact that this is a 12-byte memory write with

310
00:27:08,660 --> 00:27:12,040
where we don't control the contents makes exploitation

311
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:15,440
somewhat difficult. So the first thing that's a little bit

312
00:27:15,440 --> 00:27:18,380
tricky is that because it's a 12-byte write, if we want to

313
00:27:18,380 --> 00:27:21,620
overwrite one word in memory, then we're necessarily

314
00:27:21,620 --> 00:27:25,320
clobbering two adjacent words to that which could potentially

315
00:27:25,320 --> 00:27:29,860
lead to instability depending on what the target is. And then

316
00:27:29,860 --> 00:27:33,460
additionally, probably more importantly, we have very minimal

317
00:27:33,460 --> 00:27:36,500
options in terms of what we can do because we have this very

318
00:27:36,500 --> 00:27:40,140
limited vocabulary of what we can cause to be written in secure

319
00:27:40,140 --> 00:27:45,340
memory. We only have zeros, a 12, and a 1. That's fairly

320
00:27:45,340 --> 00:27:50,280
limited. So the challenge here is actually coming up with a way

321
00:27:50,280 --> 00:27:53,820
of using this first exploit primitive to build a more

322
00:27:53,820 --> 00:28:00,820
flexible one that allows us to achieve our goals. Now, revisiting these secure memory

323
00:28:02,820 --> 00:28:08,820
protection functions, so we recall that QSE checks the status of memory regions by iterating

324
00:28:10,900 --> 00:28:17,820
over this linked list which has hard coded regions and each of these regions is encapsulated

325
00:28:17,820 --> 00:28:23,820
by a structure that is similar to this. This is just what I reverse engineered. Each region

326
00:28:23,820 --> 00:28:27,820
structure has an ID which is just an incremental identifier.

327
00:28:27,820 --> 00:28:30,660
Flags that indicate the actual protection attributes of that

328
00:28:30,660 --> 00:28:36,500
region and then the start and end of these regions. So what I

329
00:28:36,500 --> 00:28:40,100
came up with was that we can actually use our 12 byte right

330
00:28:40,100 --> 00:28:44,340
to overwrite the flags start and end addresses of the region

331
00:28:44,340 --> 00:28:48,720
entry corresponding to the QSEE image. So the result of this

332
00:28:48,720 --> 00:28:53,820
is anytime QSEE goes to check the safety of a pointer provided

333
00:28:53,820 --> 00:28:57,460
the kernel to guarantee that it's safe for it to assume that it's

334
00:28:57,460 --> 00:28:59,860
non-secure and that it can read from it and write to it without

335
00:28:59,860 --> 00:29:04,500
consequence. If that pointer resides in the address space

336
00:29:04,500 --> 00:29:08,000
reserved for the QSE image it's going to say it's fine. It's

337
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:13,400
safe. And at this point this gives us the ability to write to

338
00:29:13,400 --> 00:29:17,580
arbitrary secure memory with any value that QSE writes as output

339
00:29:17,580 --> 00:29:22,480
at any point. Here we can basically choose a new write

340
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:26,020
primitive. We've expanded our vocabulary for what we're able to

341
00:29:26,020 --> 00:29:29,520
write to secure memory. So at this point I enumerated all of the

342
00:29:29,520 --> 00:29:33,320
SMC handlers, eliminated those that don't write any output at

343
00:29:33,320 --> 00:29:37,900
any point. And now we've got a list of things that we can write

344
00:29:37,900 --> 00:29:41,100
to arbitrary secure memory. And we can, depending on how we

345
00:29:41,100 --> 00:29:44,100
design the rest of the exploit, just pick the one that suits the

346
00:29:44,100 --> 00:29:48,180
task at hand. And now the only missing piece here is actually

347
00:29:48,180 --> 00:29:51,380
finding a way to win. You know, we have a more flexible write

348
00:29:51,380 --> 00:29:57,780
primitive but how do we actually get code execution? Well,

349
00:29:57,780 --> 00:30:02,520
revisiting how QSE actually handles SMC instructions. So

350
00:30:02,520 --> 00:30:06,620
when an SMC instruction is issued, QSE will parse the

351
00:30:06,620 --> 00:30:11,240
command structure and it will iterate over a table with these

352
00:30:11,240 --> 00:30:15,280
variable length entries where each entry corresponds to an

353
00:30:15,280 --> 00:30:20,180
SMC handler, sort of like a system call handler, where each

354
00:30:20,180 --> 00:30:24,180
entry contains an SMC number which is just the identifier of

355
00:30:24,180 --> 00:30:28,980
the SMC request. A pointer to a string describing the handler

356
00:30:28,980 --> 00:30:31,620
which was incredibly useful when trying to figure out what each

357
00:30:31,620 --> 00:30:36,120
of these handlers actually did. Various flags. And then a

358
00:30:36,120 --> 00:30:39,540
function pointer to the actual function that gets invoked by

359
00:30:39,540 --> 00:30:44,580
QSE when this SMC request matching this ID is issued. A

360
00:30:44,580 --> 00:30:48,140
number of arguments field and then a variable length array

361
00:30:48,140 --> 00:30:51,180
whose length depends on this number of arguments field because

362
00:30:51,180 --> 00:30:56,840
this variable length array is actually contains one entry for

363
00:30:56,840 --> 00:30:59,460
each argument and each entry corresponds to the length of

364
00:30:59,460 --> 00:31:06,460
each argument. So I ended up using the ability to write

365
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:14,100
essentially arbitrary data to arbitrary QSE memory to create

366
00:31:14,100 --> 00:31:18,240
what I call an SMC table extension attack. So I actually use

367
00:31:18,240 --> 00:31:22,500
arbitrary secure memory to modify the number of arguments field

368
00:31:22,500 --> 00:31:28,180
in one of the SMC tables. Table entries rather. Now what I did

369
00:31:28,180 --> 00:31:32,660
was I actually expanded the size of one of these SMC entries so

370
00:31:32,660 --> 00:31:37,460
that it's particularly massive and that when QSE is iterating

371
00:31:37,460 --> 00:31:42,160
through this table and tries to find the location of the next

372
00:31:42,160 --> 00:31:47,240
SMC entry it will actually jump way into non secure attacker

373
00:31:47,240 --> 00:31:52,000
controlled memory. At which point we can put a fake entry

374
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:56,840
there and at this point we control an SMC handler. We can

375
00:31:56,840 --> 00:32:01,780
make up an SMC ID, point the function pointer anywhere we want

376
00:32:01,780 --> 00:32:08,020
in secure memory and craft any sort of arbitrary arguments and

377
00:32:08,020 --> 00:32:12,200
basically call any function in trust zone at whim with any

378
00:32:12,200 --> 00:32:15,600
arguments we want which in my opinion is a fairly elegant way

379
00:32:15,600 --> 00:32:21,460
to gain total control of trust zone. At this point we have

380
00:32:21,460 --> 00:32:26,880
arbitrary trust zone code execution and we have one. One

381
00:32:26,880 --> 00:32:29,240
of the fun things you can do is actually find the mem copy

382
00:32:29,240 --> 00:32:32,940
implementation in the trust zone kernel and actually just

383
00:32:32,940 --> 00:32:36,340
invoke that to copy all of secure memory to a non secure

384
00:32:36,340 --> 00:32:42,040
buffer. So this would allow you to steal any DRM or secure key

385
00:32:42,040 --> 00:32:45,480
storage related stuff. Any sensitive data stored in secure

386
00:32:45,480 --> 00:32:49,160
memory now becomes available to the attacker. You could disable

387
00:32:49,160 --> 00:32:51,780
TEMA. You could disable any piece of security related

388
00:32:51,780 --> 00:32:55,020
functionality and trust zone. You have full control. And then

389
00:32:55,020 --> 00:32:59,160
perhaps the flashiest thing you can do is invoke OEM specific

390
00:32:59,160 --> 00:33:01,600
functionality to do things like permanently unlock a boot

391
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:06,340
loader of a device. So that's what I'm going to do right now.

