00:36:40,380 --> 00:36:48,380
image to analyze in the first place. Next, parsing of complex data structures, in this case these

433
00:36:48,380 --> 00:36:54,380
command buffers seem like a fairly obvious point of failure. So as a vulnerability researcher

434
00:36:54,380 --> 00:37:00,380
places like this are really good targets for scrutiny. The next thing is as a researcher

435
00:37:00,380 --> 00:37:06,380
it's important to learn to develop a spider sense and learn how to trust your gut. If it looks

436
00:37:06,380 --> 00:37:12,260
sketchy it very likely is. In this case the minute I saw in the assembly code this reversal of

437
00:37:12,260 --> 00:37:16,920
arguments in the case of an erroneous call that seemed to yell out at me as something that was

438
00:37:16,920 --> 00:37:21,260
potentially bad even though I didn't know immediately what that would be and it turned

439
00:37:21,260 --> 00:37:28,260
out to be a very critical vulnerability. Next just looking at for example boot order design

440
00:37:28,260 --> 00:37:33,140
single points of failure are a bad idea. When you look at Samsung's implementation of secure

441
00:37:33,140 --> 00:37:35,880
boot, for example, there's no single point of failure, there's

442
00:37:35,880 --> 00:37:39,440
no off switch that allows you to exploit something and unlock

443
00:37:39,440 --> 00:37:43,220
the boot loader. In contrast to Motorola, which made a design

444
00:37:43,220 --> 00:37:45,680
decision that makes it easier for them because now they don't

445
00:37:45,680 --> 00:37:48,820
have to ship different software or hardware for different

446
00:37:48,820 --> 00:37:53,160
carriers, but they also included a mechanism by which any single

447
00:37:53,160 --> 00:37:56,400
trust zone vulnerability will result in a permanent unlock of

448
00:37:56,400 --> 00:37:59,700
Motorola boot loader. Which is great if you're like me and you

449
00:37:59,700 --> 00:38:03,040
like installing stuff on your phone but not so good for Motorola's

450
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:09,040
customers. Next, I think the basic idea and design of trust zone

451
00:38:09,040 --> 00:38:15,580
is excellent. Having hardware enforced security boundaries and

452
00:38:15,580 --> 00:38:19,380
a minimal core operating system component that is considered

453
00:38:19,380 --> 00:38:25,500
trusted is a great idea. But unfortunately what I think we've

454
00:38:25,500 --> 00:38:29,500
been seeing here is that so many vendors are so jazzed about

455
00:38:29,500 --> 00:38:34,000
trust zone that there has been so much feature bloat that the

456
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:37,580
attack surface in trust zone itself has widened substantially

457
00:38:37,580 --> 00:38:40,080
and that sort of eliminates one of the big advantages of having

458
00:38:40,080 --> 00:38:43,680
a system like trust zone which is designed to be small. And then

459
00:38:43,680 --> 00:38:46,560
finally I think everyone in here already knew this but the best

460
00:38:46,560 --> 00:38:49,020
marketing team in the world will not change the fact that

461
00:38:49,020 --> 00:38:51,620
software is written by humans and humans occasionally make

462
00:38:51,620 --> 00:38:54,860
mistakes. So despite the fact that something may be billed as

463
00:38:54,860 --> 00:38:59,360
100% secure. And no joke I have actually seen that in media

464
00:38:59,360 --> 00:39:03,200
reports about trust zone. That does not mean that it's not

465
00:39:03,200 --> 00:39:05,440
worthy of attention and scrutiny and there aren't bugs

466
00:39:05,440 --> 00:39:11,440
there. That's it. So I had a contact slide. But that's all

467
00:39:11,440 --> 00:39:14,940
right. That's the end of my talk. Thank you.

468
00:39:24,860 --> 00:39:54,840
Thank you.

